wine-sg (10.0-1131) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Every user's hive and session is enforced, however many users there
    are (9151). The descriptors that keep each user's HKCU theirs were
    kept in a table of 32: from the 33rd user on, a hive's keys were open
    to every account again. Users' sessions were kept in a table of 200,
    and every later user was given SYSTEM's session. Both have no limit
    now, and when the server is out of memory a hive is checked against
    SYSTEM and Administrators only and a user still gets a session of
    their own. HKEY_USERS itself and HKEY_USERS\.Default (the template of
    every new profile) had no descriptor -- any account could create a
    hive there for someone who had not logged on yet, or change .Default
    -- and now have the machine keys' (SYSTEM and Administrators full,
    the users' read), as on Windows. Gate test-manyusers; test-domainsid
    now expects a domain user's key to be owned by that user (9150).

 -- Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>  Mon, 23 Nov 2026 21:22:48 -0800

wine-sg (10.0-1130) unstable; urgency=medium

  * What an elevated program creates is Administrators', as on Windows
    (9150). Keys an elevated installer wrote (Uninstall entries, its
    HKLM\Software keys) granted the installing person, who could change
    and delete them unelevated; and every token made this machine's
    Domain Users -- held by every account -- the owner of what it created,
    so a standard user could open HKLM\Software\Classes and an admin's
    installer keys for WRITE_DAC and DELETE. Now the owner is
    Administrators for an administrator's full token (elevated, SYSTEM)
    and the user otherwise; an elevated token's default DACL gives SYSTEM
    and Administrators (and the users' read), not the person. Keys of a
    user's hive without a descriptor are now checked against the user's
    descriptor, not only reported with it: another standard user could
    create keys in someone's HKCU\Software. That descriptor gives
    RESTRICTED and ALL APPLICATION PACKAGES read, as Windows' HKCU. An
    elevated program's process and threads give the person only
    SYNCHRONIZE and the limited query: any program run unelevated could
    open the person's elevated program and write its memory. People keep
    their own things: keys an elevated program makes in their HKCU or
    their own Installer\UserData stay theirs, also when it later sets only
    their owner. Key descriptors are not saved, so earlier elevated keys
    are back to the machine default at the next server restart. Gate
    test-elevobj (7 mutants).

